# Add scope-aware API tokens

## Summary

Adds scope-aware authorization for user-owned API tokens.

This PR introduces a durable token-scope vocabulary, stores scopes on personal API tokens, exposes scopes through the token create/list APIs, carries token identity and scopes through request authentication, and enforces required scopes on build, log, artifact, and build-run style API operations.

This is the security prerequisite for future CLI build inspection, log retrieval, artifact access, and rerun commands.

## What changed

### Token scope vocabulary

Added a small durable scope set:

- `build:read` — read build metadata and status.
- `build:logs` — read build logs and failed-step diagnostics.
- `build:run` — trigger build/run/retry/rerun style actions.
- `artifact:read` — list or read build artifacts.

Scopes are normalized by trimming, deduplicating, validating against the allowed vocabulary, and storing in deterministic sorted order.

### Conservative migration

Added a scopes column for API tokens.

Existing tokens are backfilled with an empty scope set, so they do not silently gain resource access.

Existing valid tokens can still authenticate to identity endpoints such as `/api/me`, but they cannot read builds, logs, artifacts, or trigger build mutations unless scopes are explicitly granted.

### Token APIs

Updated the current-user token create/list contract to include scopes.

Token creation now:

- accepts requested scopes;
- rejects unknown scopes;
- canonicalizes scope ordering;
- persists scopes;
- returns scope metadata;
- still returns the raw token secret only once on creation.

Token listing now returns safe token metadata including scopes, without exposing token secrets or hashes.

### Authentication principal

Extended authenticated request context for API-token callers.

The request principal now carries:

- authentication method;
- token ID;
- token scopes.

Session/browser authentication behavior remains unchanged.

Valid API tokens can still authenticate to `/api/me`, which keeps CLI `auth status` working for all valid personal tokens.

### Scope enforcement

Added centralized scope authorization helpers.

For session-authenticated requests, existing user/project authorization remains the authority and token scopes are not required.

For API-token-authenticated requests, protected resource operations now require both:

```text
owning user is authorized for the resource
AND
token includes the required scope
```

Scope checks were applied to build, job, and artifact handlers, including:

- build metadata/status reads;
- log reads;
- artifact reads;
- create/run-now/retry/rerun and related build mutations.

A token with a scope still cannot exceed the owning user's normal permissions.

### Personal API-token UI

Updated the personal token UI to support scopes.

The page now:

- displays scopes for existing tokens;
- allows selecting scopes during token creation;
- defaults to a least-privilege diagnostic token with `build:read` and `build:logs`;
- leaves `build:run` unselected by default;
- preserves the one-time token secret display behavior.

This supports safe creation of IDE-agent diagnostic tokens without granting rerun capability by default.

### Documentation and Swagger

Regenerated Swagger for the token contract updates.

Updated AI-context/token documentation to describe:

- scope vocabulary;
- token scope storage;
- request principal scope handling;
- protected route scope enforcement;
- personal token UI scope selection;
- CLI build/log/rerun commands as the next deferred slice.

## Validation

Ran focused backend and frontend tests covering:

- scope vocabulary and normalization;
- token creation/listing with scopes;
- unknown scope rejection;
- conservative existing-token behavior;
- token authentication principal scope attachment;
- `/api/me` with token authentication;
- revoked/expired/malformed token behavior;
- scope enforcement for API-token callers;
- unchanged browser/session behavior;
- build/job/artifact handler authorization behavior;
- personal token UI scope selection and display.

Commands run:

```bash
go test ./internal/service ./internal/repository/memory ./internal/repository/postgres ./internal/auth ./internal/http/handler -run 'Test(APIToken|Middleware|Authorize|ScopedAPIToken|BuildHandler|ArtifactHandler|JobHandler|UserHandler)'
go test ./internal/auth ./internal/http/handler -run 'Test(Middleware|UserHandler|APITokenHandler|Authorize|ScopedAPIToken)'
npm test -- --run src/pages/APITokensPage.test.tsx
make swagger
```

## Deferred

Intentionally out of scope:

- `coyote build status`;
- `coyote build logs`;
- `coyote build rerun`;
- artifact download CLI commands;
- Slack command text updates;
- service-account tokens;
- admin impersonation;
- OIDC/device login;
- token editing;
- broad role or permission redesign.

